y wouldn't reach a conclusion  and I think that perhaps you will find  economists are having different views  about the future I think the majority of  people who follow this closely today  however have gotten over the depression  six months ago and now the debate rather  is the rate at which we're going to grow  over the next period ahead well how much  of a role dr. Haga is the federal  government actually played can you  create a inflation or a deflation or  that up to the businessman on a consumer  the federal government of course if it  wants to pursue radically altered  policies such as trying to for example  unbalanced its budget very rapidly by  billions of dollars of spending in  various ways and by pursuing and  exceedingly unwise monetary policy I  think could could make an inflation  rather quickly and similarly if it  wanted to pursue monetary policies and  budgetary policies have a radically  constricted the restrictive type could  have a profound effect but I think by  and large the economy of America is  increasingly going to be dominated and  directed by the millions of Americans  all throughout this land who are  confident about the future and whose  decisions are going to aggregate into a  growing reasonably stable economy and if  the if the if the government can pursue  right policies in that area I think  we're going to have a fine period of it  thank you very much dr. huggies been  very interesting to have you here  tonight the opinions expressed on the  launching Kronus cope with those of the  speakers the editorial board for this  edition of the law and gene Cronus cope  was Larry lesser and Winston Burdette  our distinguished guest was dr. Gabriel  Hoggy economic adviser to the president
 

